Your target is clear: a cinematic black hole with a luminous accretion disk, not a conventional spiral galaxy.
Looking at your screenshot, I'd keep the nearly pure-black background and the blue particle aesthetic. Those already give you a strong foundation. The biggest change is to make the black hole feel like a massive object bending light and heating matter, rather than a small dark spot surrounded by a cloud of particles.

The visual direction

9

Aim for this visual hierarchy:
First glance: a clearly defined, pitch-black event horizon.
Second glance: an intense, thin, glowing accretion disk wrapping around it.
Third glance: particles, plasma turbulence, and subtle gravitational lensing.
Background: almost entirely black, with sparse stars so the black hole dominates.
Your current image spreads attention across the entire particle cloud. The revised composition should concentrate attention on the center.
2. Redesign the composition
A. Event horizon — the focal point

https://t.co/BsGtbLnfEu
Make the central silhouette clearly visible, with a clean dark interior.
Add a thin, intensely bright photon-ring effect around the silhouette.
Keep the ring crisp; avoid turning the entire center into a white blob.
The black hole should occupy roughly 10–16% of the image width as a starting composition target, adjusted for the disk's apparent scale.
B. Accretion disk — the main spectacle

https://t.co/GnuVvEZrgi
Replace the uniform oval particle cloud with a thin, structured disk.
Use a hot white-gold inner region and cooler blue-cyan outer material.
Create density bands, uneven clumps, and subtle turbulence instead of evenly distributed dots.
Tilt the disk relative to the camera so the near and far sides are visually distinguishable.
C. Gravitational lensing — the signature effect

https://t.co/bTX3J8Y49X
Bend the far side of the disk visually around the black hole, creating an arc above the silhouette.
Keep the lower and upper arcs connected to the same disk; they should not look like separate floating rings.
Add a restrained distortion of the background behind the event horizon.
3. Technical implementation plan
Build this as separate rendering layers rather than trying to make one particle system do everything.
Layer 5 — Background
Black space · sparse stars · subtle distant haze
Layer 4 — Accretion-disk particles
Orbital paths · density variation · temperature colors
Layer 3 — Plasma and emission
Inner glow · turbulent filaments · controlled bloom
Layer 2 — Lensing and photon ring
Distortion pass · luminous arcs · dark silhouette
Final composite
Exposure · contrast · color grading · vignette
A. Rebuild the particle distribution
Your screenshot's particle cloud has a fairly smooth oval boundary and an even spread. Give the particles a disk-shaped distribution with a clear inner edge, outer edge, and density variation.
For a simple flat disk, sample the orbital radius using:
\[
r=\sqrt{r_{\min}^2+u(r_{\max}^2-r_{\min}^2)}
\]
where \(u\) is a uniform random number between 0 and
This produces a uniform distribution over disk area. Then adjust particle density to concentrate more material near the inner disk if desired.
For each particle, store:
Orbital radius \(r\)
Orbital angle \(\theta\)
Orbital speed or angular velocity
Particle brightness and size
Temperature or color variation
A small, slowly changing turbulence offset
For a Newtonian orbital approximation, angular velocity scales as:
\[
\omega(r)\propto r^{-3/2}
\]
So the inner particles move faster than the outer ones. This gives the disk a much more coherent motion than applying random movement to every particle. For a physically accurate black hole, the inner-orbit behavior requires relativistic corrections; this formula is a starting approximation, not a complete black-hole model.
B. Make the light feel hot
Use a combination of emissive particle colors and a separate glow pass.
Region
Suggested appearance
Outer disk
Deep blue, cyan
Mid-disk
Blue-white, pale violet
Inner disk
Gold, amber, white
Photon ring
Thin, near-white glow
Event horizon
Nearly pure black
Use additive blending for the brightest emission, but keep the main particle layer relatively sharp. Apply bloom after the scene is rendered rather than giving every particle an enormous soft halo.
Start with a restrained bloom radius and strength, then increase them until the inner disk looks luminous without losing its structure. The precise values depend on your renderer and exposure settings.
C. Add gravitational lensing
This is the effect most likely to make the simulation feel extraordinary.
A physically convincing black hole bends light from the far side of the disk around the event horizon, so an observer can see part of the disk above the black hole as well as across the front.
For a cinematic approximation:
Render the background and disk to a texture.
Use a screen-space shader to distort the sampled image around the black hole's projected position.
Increase the distortion as pixels approach the black hole.
Add a separate photon-ring glow and preserve the dark event-horizon silhouette.
A simple radial warp can create a stylized effect, but it will not reproduce actual relativistic ray tracing. For more realistic results, trace rays through a Schwarzschild or Kerr spacetime model, or use precomputed lensing maps.
D. Animate it without making it chaotic
Your simulation should feel powerful, not frantic.
Keep the camera fixed.
Rotate the disk slowly, with the inner material moving faster than the outer material.
Let turbulence evolve gradually rather than flickering randomly.
Add occasional subtle brightness variations along the disk.
Keep the background stars almost motionless.
If you use particle trails, make them faint and short enough to preserve a clean image.
The goal is to make the light and matter feel like they are flowing continuously around an immense gravitational object.
4. Concrete rendering targets
Use these as starting targets rather than strict physical specifications.
Composition
60–75%
of the frame width occupied by the main disk and its glow
Background
85–95%
visually dark, preserving negative space
Motion
Slow
continuous orbital movement, no camera shake
Lighting
High contrast
sharp core with controlled surrounding glow
For performance, use GPU-driven particles or instanced rendering where possible. Avoid creating a separate draw call for every particle. Render the glow and lensing effects in a small number of post-processing passes, and scale the effect resolution independently if needed.
5. Implement in this order
Composition first: enlarge and clearly frame the black hole, and make the disk's shape recognizable.
Particle structure second: establish the inner and outer disk, orbital distribution, and density bands.
Lighting third: add emissive inner material, a photon ring, and carefully tuned bloom.
Lensing fourth: warp the far-side disk and background around the black hole.
Motion fifth: implement differential rotation and subtle turbulence.
Polish last: adjust exposure, color balance, framing, and performance.
Don't add all the effects at once. Compare screenshots after each step; otherwise, you won't know which change actually improves the result.
