Treat these as starting points. The final result depends on the renderer's color space, tone mapping, and the brightness of your shader output.
Three adjustments will matter most:
If the center becomes a white blob: reduce bloom intensity or increase the luminance threshold.
If the disk looks like neon dots: reduce particle brightness and let the continuous shader carry more of the light.
If the scene looks washed out: reduce the overall emission and preserve more nearly black space.
Also, make sure your camera is framed around the entire disk rather than just the event horizon. In your original screenshot, the particle field is very wide relative to the central object. A tighter composition will help immediately.
7. Performance and visual debugging
Don't optimize only by counting particles. Profile the GPU and rendering passes, particularly at your target resolution.
GPU performance
Keep particle attributes in typed arrays, avoid rebuilding geometry on each render, and update shader uniforms rather than React state every frame.
Transparency and draw order
Transparent objects can still render in unexpected orders. Use a consistent disk orientation, depth testing, and render order. Avoid relying on renderOrder alone to make overlapping transparent layers physically correct.
Debug one effect at a time
Temporarily disable bloom, then lensing, then particles. If the disk looks good without post-processing but bad with it, fix the post-processing rather than changing the particle simulation.
8. Your implementation roadmap
Milestone 1 — composition: add the dark sphere, continuous disk, and aligned ring. Get a good static screenshot.
Milestone 2 — motion: replace uniform rotation with the GPU orbital shader. Verify that inner particles move faster than outer particles.
Milestone 3 — lighting: tune emission and bloom until the inner disk glows without losing detail.
Milestone 4 — lensing: add the screen-space approximation, then replace it with a more physical method if needed.
Milestone 5 — polish: tune the camera, color grading, and background. Check performance at your target screen resolution.
My recommendation: implement Milestone 1 first and don't touch the particle count until the static composition looks compelling. A beautifully shaped, well-lit disk with 5,000 carefully designed particles will often look more convincing than a uniform cloud of 100,000 particles.
